1
00:00:03.351 --> 00:00:03.400
Now, there's a lot of agents being built across the world.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.199
A lot of them are already online.

3
00:00:05.199 --> 00:00:09.279
And sometimes you need your agents to collaborate with other agents in the web.

4
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:13.520
And A2A is a protocol that has been getting a lot of traction, and many people are starting

5
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.520
using it.

6
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.880
So let's take a look into that.

7
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.920
So as we talk about protocols before, when we discussed MCPs, there is a new protocol

8
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.459
that is coming up to allow agents to talk to other agents.

9
00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:29.000
And that's important because we're talking about agents' communication.

10
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.500
With MCP, you give your agents the ability to call external tools.

11
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.740
And with A2A, you're giving your agents the ability to call external agents.

12
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.580
This is a brand new protocol.

13
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.979
It has not been around for long, but it is starting to get some traction.

14
00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:48.740
And it's already supported by CrewAI and many other frameworks and vendors as well.

15
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.459
It's an open standard, the same way that MCP is, and it tries to anchor itself around

16
00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:57.419
a few different key principles.

17
00:00:57.419 --> 00:01:02.000
It has the idea of making things extremely simple, using the same idea of JSON-RPC and

18
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.040
SSE as MCP did.

19
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.519
And it tries to make sure that it follows strict guidelines for auth, security, and

20
00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:10.519
monitoring.

21
00:01:10.519 --> 00:01:15.120
The idea here is that you can have these executions happen and have this agent exchange messages

22
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.459
with each other, but not only giving them tasks, but also messages and artifacts.

23
00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:26.639
So if you look at individual components of A2A, you're going to find that it's all about

24
00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:28.720
these main pieces.

25
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.400
An agent card, and the idea here is basically identify the agent, its capabilities, what

26
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.199
it's able to do, any requirements that it might have.

27
00:01:38.199 --> 00:01:42.360
For the task side, it's very similar to the way that we have tasks in Crew.

28
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.199
It's a stateful unit of work where you're delegating something that you expect this

29
00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:47.480
agent to do.

30
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.720
The message is the interaction between these agents as they talk with each other.

31
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:59.720
And the artifact is the actual tangible output that was generated as a byproduct of the task

32
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.199
execution.

33
00:02:01.199 --> 00:02:05.839
You can already see how it maps to a lot of the things that we talked in CrewAI up to

34
00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:11.520
this point, and that justifies a lot of the traction that this A2A protocol has been getting.

35
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.479
The ability that creates this common layer that allows many different agents from different

36
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:19.119
technologies to talk to each other.

37
00:02:19.119 --> 00:02:24.080
So I do want to make sure that I drive the difference here between MCP and A2A.

38
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:31.360
With MCP, you're allowing your LLMs or agents to connect to these external tools and stateless

39
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.600
datasets, for example, prompts and such.

40
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.600
And with A2A, what you're getting more is actually having one agent to trigger another

41
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.600
agent.

42
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.240
So they can not only talk to each other, but they can get the work done and eventually

43
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.679
report back.

44
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.839
So the key distinction here is that wrapping agents as tools fundamentally changes their

45
00:02:51.839 --> 00:02:52.839
capabilities.

46
00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:59.000
If you try to wrap an agent alone under MCP, that doesn't necessarily explore the full

47
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.639
extent to which you can actually use that agent.

48
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:08.440
And A2A is more thinking from the ground up to support agents natively instead of making

49
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.240
them as tools.

50
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.440
Both works, but A2As are definitely getting more traction nowadays.

51
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:20.119
Now the relationship of A2A complements MCP, so it's not like one or the other.

52
00:03:20.119 --> 00:03:24.839
You probably will find yourself using both in a few use cases, depending on what kind

53
00:03:24.839 --> 00:03:27.360
of external integrations you have.

54
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.539
So there are two integration approaches to use A2A with CrewAI.

55
00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:35.919
The first one is by exposing CrewAI agents as A2A servers.

56
00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:40.039
So you make your crew discoverable and callable by external agents.

57
00:03:40.039 --> 00:03:45.639
And the other way is by consuming these external A2A agents as tools, enabling your crew agents

58
00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:49.759
to delegate tasks to these other A2A compliant agents.

59
00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:51.720
The first pattern is extremely simple.

60
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.880
You're basically wrapping up your crew behind an HTTP interface that speaks the A2A protocol.

61
00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.660
So basically having an HTTP protocol.

62
00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.960
And the architecture components are extremely straightforward.

63
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.559
It's using the same HTTP and JSON RPC structure that we talked about during MCP.

64
00:04:08.559 --> 00:04:14.119
The second pattern is actually allowing your agents to call remote A2A agents from your

65
00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:15.600
CrewAI workflow.

66
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.119
And that allows you to do it through a custom tool, where you can basically call an A2A

67
00:04:20.119 --> 00:04:21.119
remote server.

68
00:04:21.119 --> 00:04:23.160
And that is extremely straightforward.

69
00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.760
Now the point that I want to drive across here with A2A is that, as you can see, the

70
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.079
industry is trying to get together and figure out what are the standards that we as an industry

71
00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:35.079
wants to follow.

72
00:04:35.079 --> 00:04:37.119
And there are many options right now.

73
00:04:37.119 --> 00:04:42.839
It's an entire new industry and many companies and many developers and many teams are trying

74
00:04:42.839 --> 00:04:48.320
to figure out the most responsible and efficient way to get these agents to talk to each other

75
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.959
and get these agents to use tools across the wire.

76
00:04:51.959 --> 00:04:56.760
What you can find in A2A is a very premium protocol that has been scaling and getting

77
00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.720
adoption in order to allow agents to talk with other agents on the internet.

78
00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.679
It's still unclear whether there's going to be one single protocol that's going to rule

79
00:05:05.679 --> 00:05:07.399
all the agents out there.

80
00:05:07.399 --> 00:05:12.119
But A2A is definitely one example of one that is getting a lot of traction and that already

81
00:05:12.119 --> 00:05:14.200
integrates and works with CrewAI.

82
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.399
And I want to make sure to highlight that.

